--TEST--
`purge`'s four selectors are one trailing options shape, so a state is written inside it and never as a second argument — `rule:concurrency/queue-deletion-is-explicit-and-bounded`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every selector this member has is a field of the one shape, written in any
// order and each of them optional: what it removes is decided by what the call
// wrote, and a call that wrote nothing removes what has finished.
uint $finished = Core\Queue::purge("email");
uint $dead = Core\Queue::purge("email", {state: Core\Queue\State::Dead, limit: 100});
uint $old = Core\Queue::purge("email", {tag: "import", limit: 50});

// And there is no positional half to it. A member that also took its state
// beside the queue would be two spellings of one call, which is what R3's
// single trailing shape exists to prevent.
uint $beside = Core\Queue::purge("email", Core\Queue\State::Dead);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0453]: an options argument must be written out as `{...}` at the call site
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
